1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:02,364
Section three: sources.

2
00:00:02,644 --> 00:00:09,748
This is how we tell dbt about data that already exists in the database, data that dbt did not build.

3
00:00:10,847 --> 00:00:13,877
One rule: never hard-code a table name in a model.

4
00:00:14,157 --> 00:00:17,101
It works, but dbt cannot see the dependency.

5
00:00:17,381 --> 00:00:23,653
It cannot draw it in the lineage, test it, or document it, and if the schema changes, every model breaks.

6
00:00:24,753 --> 00:00:26,758
On the left, a hard-coded table.

7
00:00:27,038 --> 00:00:30,772
On the right, the source function, with the source name and the table name.

8
00:00:31,052 --> 00:00:38,284
Both compile to the same SQL, but only the right one appears in the lineage graph, with tests and descriptions attached.

9
00:00:39,384 --> 00:00:41,261
The sources page, version one.

10
00:00:41,541 --> 00:00:45,360
Declare sources in YAML, with a name, the schema, and the tables.

11
00:00:45,640 --> 00:00:47,752
Select from them with the source function.

12
00:00:48,032 --> 00:00:50,528
Add tests and descriptions to source columns.

13
00:00:50,808 --> 00:00:54,349
And freshness can warn you when a loader stops delivering data.

14
00:00:55,449 --> 00:01:00,057
On our lineage graph, the first node lights up: the source for yellow taxi trips.

15
00:01:00,337 --> 00:01:03,238
Everything else in the project will be built on top of it.

16
00:01:04,338 --> 00:01:06,898
Rosetta can generate the source file for us.

17
00:01:07,178 --> 00:01:12,170
The Raw Layer option reads the database and writes a YAML file with every column.

18
00:01:12,450 --> 00:01:13,666
No AI involved.

19
00:01:13,946 --> 00:01:16,677
It also adds a not null test to every column.

20
00:01:16,957 --> 00:01:19,410
That is a guess, and real data will test it.

21
00:01:20,510 --> 00:01:29,833
In the demo, the agent moves that file into our staging folder, names the source T L C, and adds a description to every column from the dictionary file.

22
00:01:30,113 --> 00:01:32,182
We tell it not to change the tests yet.

23
00:01:33,282 --> 00:01:35,501
This is what we review before keeping it.

24
00:01:35,781 --> 00:01:37,317
The source name and schema.

25
00:01:37,597 --> 00:01:39,688
Descriptions that match the dictionary.

26
00:01:39,968 --> 00:01:44,234
Notice payment type starts at zero, Flex Fare, from the current dictionary.

27
00:01:44,514 --> 00:01:46,861
And the tests are still the generated guesses.

28
00:01:47,961 --> 00:01:49,476
Then we run the source tests.

29
00:01:49,756 --> 00:01:56,348
Several not null tests fail: passenger count, rate code, the store and forward flag, and some surcharges.

30
00:01:56,628 --> 00:01:59,038
That is real data pushing back on a guess.

31
00:02:00,138 --> 00:02:02,506
Instead of deleting the tests, we ask why.

32
00:02:02,786 --> 00:02:06,285
In Ask mode: which rows fail, and what do they have in common?

33
00:02:07,385 --> 00:02:14,852
In our data, the rows with an empty passenger count are the rows with payment type zero, which the dictionary calls Flex Fare.

34
00:02:15,132 --> 00:02:16,796
So these are not broken rows.

35
00:02:17,076 --> 00:02:20,532
They are a different kind of trip, where some fields are not recorded.

36
00:02:21,632 --> 00:02:22,720
So we decide.

37
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:28,930
We keep not null where a trip cannot exist without a value: the times, the locations and the total.

38
00:02:29,210 --> 00:02:32,602
We drop it where the source legitimately leaves values empty.

39
00:02:32,882 --> 00:02:35,293
A failing test is a decision, not a bug.

40
00:02:36,393 --> 00:02:37,993
One last thing: freshness.

41
00:02:38,273 --> 00:02:40,662
It compares a timestamp with the current time.

42
00:02:40,942 --> 00:02:44,846
Our data is historical, so it would always look stale, and we skip it.

43
00:02:45,126 --> 00:02:48,006
In a real project with daily data, you turn it on.

44
00:02:48,286 --> 00:02:50,505
It tells you when a loader stopped working.
